## raymathx.pengu
## Layer-2 wrapper over the `raymath` binding: Pengunic methods for vectors, quaternions and matrices.
##
## The wrapper adds no storage and no types of its own beyond two small result `rune`s
## (`AxisAngle`, `MatrixParts`). Every method lowers to one direct call into
## `pengu/raymath.d.pengu`; the types (`Vector2`, `Vector3`, `Vector4`, `Quaternion`,
## `Matrix`) are declared in `pengu/raylib.d.pengu` and are only extended here with
## `enchanting`.
##
## `Quaternion` is an alias of `Vector4`, so both names share one method table. All the
## arithmetic lives on `raylib.Vector4`; `raylib.Quaternion` adds only the operations that
## are meaningful for a rotation (`mul`, `slerp_to`, `nlerp_to`, `cubic_hermite_to`,
## `to_matrix`, `to_euler`, `to_axis_angle`). Never declare the same method name in both
## blocks: the compiler would emit one C function twice and the C build would fail.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation, no ownership, nothing to release: raymath is by-value arithmetic. There is
## no `banish` and no `std.ffi` in this module.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## import raylib
## import raymathx
##
## weave main into int:
##     var v as raylib.Vector3 is calling Vector3.new with 3.0, 4.0, 0.0
##     calling v.normalize
##     var n as f32 is calling v.length
##     return 0
## ```

import raylib
import raymath
import std.ward

## The rotation axis and angle extracted from a quaternion.
##
## ## Invariants
## - `axis` is normalized.
## - `angle` is in radians.
rune AxisAngle:
    axis as raylib.Vector3
    angle as f32

## The translation, rotation and scale decomposed from a transform matrix.
##
## ## Invariants
## - `rotation` is a unit quaternion.
## - Any shear present in the source matrix is removed during decomposition.
rune MatrixParts:
    translation as raylib.Vector3
    rotation as raylib.Quaternion
    scale as raylib.Vector3

## Clamps a scalar between `lo` and `hi`.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `value`: the value to clamp.
## - `lo`: lower bound.
## - `hi`: upper bound.
##
## ## Returns
## `lo` if `value` is below it, `hi` if above, otherwise `value`.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var c as f32 is calling clamp with 5.0, 0.0, 1.0   # 1.0
## ```
weave clamp with value as f32, lo as f32, hi as f32 into f32:
    return calling raymath.Clamp with value, lo, hi

## Linearly interpolates between two scalars.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `start`: value at `t = 0`.
## - `end`: value at `t = 1`.
## - `t`: blend factor.
##
## ## Returns
## The interpolated value.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var m as f32 is calling lerp with 0.0, 10.0, 0.5   # 5.0
## ```
weave lerp with start as f32, end as f32, t as f32 into f32:
    return calling raymath.Lerp with start, end, t

## Normalizes a value within a range, remapping it to `[0, 1]`.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `value`: the value to normalize.
## - `start`: start of the input range.
## - `end`: end of the input range.
##
## ## Returns
## The normalized value.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var n as f32 is calling normalize_in with 5.0, 0.0, 10.0   # 0.5
## ```
weave normalize_in with value as f32, start as f32, end as f32 into f32:
    return calling raymath.Normalize with value, start, end

## Remaps a value from one range to another.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `value`: the value to remap.
## - `in_start`, `in_end`: the input range.
## - `out_start`, `out_end`: the output range.
##
## ## Returns
## The remapped value.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var r as f32 is calling remap with 5.0, 0.0, 10.0, 0.0, 100.0   # 50.0
## ```
weave remap with value as f32, in_start as f32, in_end as f32, out_start as f32, out_end as f32 into f32:
    return calling raymath.Remap with value, in_start, in_end, out_start, out_end

## Wraps a value into the `[lo, hi]` range.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `value`: the value to wrap.
## - `lo`: lower bound.
## - `hi`: upper bound.
##
## ## Returns
## The wrapped value.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var w as f32 is calling wrap with 370.0, 0.0, 360.0   # 10.0
## ```
weave wrap with value as f32, lo as f32, hi as f32 into f32:
    return calling raymath.Wrap with value, lo, hi

## Compares two scalars approximately, using raymath's `EPSILON`.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `a`, `b`: the values to compare.
##
## ## Returns
## `true` when the values are closer than `EPSILON`.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var same as bool is calling approx_eq with 0.1 + 0.2, 0.3   # true
## ```
weave approx_eq with a as f32, b as f32 into bool:
    return (calling raymath.FloatEquals with a, b) != 0

## Returns the identity quaternion.
##
## ## Returns
## A unit quaternion with `w = 1`, representing no rotation.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var q as raylib.Quaternion is calling quaternion_identity
## ```
weave quaternion_identity into raylib.Quaternion:
    return calling raymath.QuaternionIdentity

## Builds a quaternion from a rotation axis and an angle.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `axis`: the rotation axis. It does not need to be normalized.
## - `angle`: the rotation in radians.
##
## ## Returns
## The rotation quaternion.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var q as raylib.Quaternion is calling quaternion_from_axis_angle with (calling Vector3.new with 0.0, 1.0, 0.0), 1.5707963
## ```
weave quaternion_from_axis_angle with axis as raylib.Vector3, angle as f32 into raylib.Quaternion:
    return calling raymath.QuaternionFromAxisAngle with axis, angle

## Builds a quaternion from Euler angles.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `pitch`, `yaw`, `roll`: the angles in radians, applied in ZYX order.
##
## ## Returns
## The rotation quaternion.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var q as raylib.Quaternion is calling quaternion_from_euler with 0.1, 0.2, 0.3
## ```
weave quaternion_from_euler with pitch as f32, yaw as f32, roll as f32 into raylib.Quaternion:
    return calling raymath.QuaternionFromEuler with pitch, yaw, roll

## Builds a quaternion from a rotation matrix.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `mat`: the rotation matrix.
##
## ## Returns
## The rotation quaternion.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var q as raylib.Quaternion is calling quaternion_from_matrix with (calling Matrix.identity)
## ```
weave quaternion_from_matrix with mat as raylib.Matrix into raylib.Quaternion:
    return calling raymath.QuaternionFromMatrix with mat

## Builds the quaternion that rotates one vector onto another.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `from`: the source direction.
## - `to`: the destination direction.
##
## ## Returns
## The rotation quaternion.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var q as raylib.Quaternion is calling quaternion_from_vector_to_vector with (calling Vector3.new with 1.0, 0.0, 0.0), (calling Vector3.new with 0.0, 1.0, 0.0)
## ```
weave quaternion_from_vector_to_vector with from as raylib.Vector3, to as raylib.Vector3 into raylib.Quaternion:
    return calling raymath.QuaternionFromVector3ToVector3 with from, to

enchanting raylib.Vector2:

    ## Builds a 2D vector from its two components.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `x`: horizontal component.
    ## - `y`: vertical component.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## A `raylib.Vector2` with the given components.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 3.0, 4.0
    ## ```

    weave ritual new with x as f32, y as f32 into raylib.Vector2:
        return with x is x, y is y

    ## Returns the additive identity `(0, 0)`.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## A `raylib.Vector2` with both components at `0.0`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var z as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.zero
    ## ```

    weave ritual zero into raylib.Vector2:
        return calling raymath.Vector2Zero

    ## Returns the vector `(1, 1)`.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## A `raylib.Vector2` with both components at `1.0`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var o as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.one
    ## ```

    weave ritual one into raylib.Vector2:
        return calling raymath.Vector2One

    ## Builds a vector with the same value in every component.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `value`: the component to repeat.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## A `raylib.Vector2` equal to `(value, value)`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var s as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.splat with 2.5
    ## ```

    weave ritual splat with value as f32 into raylib.Vector2:
        return with x is value, y is value

    ## Adds `other` to the receiver, component-wise.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `other`: the vector to add.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The receiver, now equal to `self + other`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation; the receiver is rewritten in place.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 1.0, 2.0
    ## calling v.add with (calling Vector2.splat with 1.0)   # v is (2, 3)
    ## ```

    @mutates
    weave add with other as raylib.Vector2 into raylib.Vector2:
        return calling raymath.Vector2Add with (essence of self), other

    ## Adds a scalar to both components of the receiver.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `add`: the value to add to each component.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The receiver, now equal to `self + (add, add)`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation; the receiver is rewritten in place.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 1.0, 2.0
    ## calling v.add_scalar with 0.5   # v is (1.5, 2.5)
    ## ```

    @mutates
    weave add_scalar with add as f32 into raylib.Vector2:
        return calling raymath.Vector2AddValue with (essence of self), add

    ## Subtracts `other` from the receiver, component-wise.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `other`: the vector to subtract.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The receiver, now equal to `self - other`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation; the receiver is rewritten in place.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 3.0, 4.0
    ## calling v.subtract with (calling Vector2.splat with 1.0)   # v is (2, 3)
    ## ```

    @mutates
    weave subtract with other as raylib.Vector2 into raylib.Vector2:
        return calling raymath.Vector2Subtract with (essence of self), other

    ## Subtracts a scalar from both components of the receiver.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `sub`: the value to subtract from each component.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The receiver, now equal to `self - (sub, sub)`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation; the receiver is rewritten in place.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 1.0, 2.0
    ## calling v.subtract_scalar with 0.5   # v is (0.5, 1.5)
    ## ```

    @mutates
    weave subtract_scalar with sub as f32 into raylib.Vector2:
        return calling raymath.Vector2SubtractValue with (essence of self), sub

    ## Multiplies the receiver by `other`, component-wise.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `other`: the vector to multiply by.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The receiver, now equal to `self * other` component-wise.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation; the receiver is rewritten in place.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 2.0, 3.0
    ## calling v.multiply with (calling Vector2.splat with 2.0)   # v is (4, 6)
    ## ```

    @mutates
    weave multiply with other as raylib.Vector2 into raylib.Vector2:
        return calling raymath.Vector2Multiply with (essence of self), other

    ## Scales both components of the receiver by `factor`.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `factor`: the scalar multiplier.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The receiver, now scaled by `factor`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation; the receiver is rewritten in place.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 2.0, 3.0
    ## calling v.scale with 2.0   # v is (4, 6)
    ## ```

    @mutates
    weave scale with factor as f32 into raylib.Vector2:
        return calling raymath.Vector2Scale with (essence of self), factor

    ## Divides the receiver by `other`, component-wise.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `other`: the divisor. A zero component yields `inf` or `NaN`, as in C.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The receiver, now equal to `self / other` component-wise.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation; the receiver is rewritten in place.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 4.0, 6.0
    ## calling v.divide with (calling Vector2.splat with 2.0)   # v is (2, 3)
    ## ```

    @mutates
    weave divide with other as raylib.Vector2 into raylib.Vector2:
        return calling raymath.Vector2Divide with (essence of self), other

    ## Flips the sign of both components of the receiver.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The receiver, now equal to `-self`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation; the receiver is rewritten in place.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 1.0, -2.0
    ## calling v.negate   # v is (-1, 2)
    ## ```

    @mutates
    weave negate into raylib.Vector2:
        return calling raymath.Vector2Negate with (essence of self)

    ## Replaces each component with its reciprocal.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - A zero component yields `inf`, as in C; this is not a normalisation.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The receiver, now equal to `(1/x, 1/y)`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation; the receiver is rewritten in place.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 2.0, 4.0
    ## calling v.invert   # v is (0.5, 0.25)
    ## ```

    @mutates
    weave invert into raylib.Vector2:
        return calling raymath.Vector2Invert with (essence of self)

    ## Normalises the receiver to unit length.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - The zero vector is returned unchanged (length `0`), so no division by zero occurs.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The receiver, now of length `1` (or still zero for the zero vector).
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation; the receiver is rewritten in place.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 3.0, 4.0
    ## calling v.normalize   # v is (0.6, 0.8)
    ## ```

    @mutates
    weave normalize into raylib.Vector2:
        return calling raymath.Vector2Normalize with (essence of self)

    ## Clamps each component of the receiver between `lo` and `hi`.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `lo`: per-component lower bound.
    ## - `hi`: per-component upper bound.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The receiver, with every component inside `[lo, hi]`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation; the receiver is rewritten in place.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 5.0, -5.0
    ## calling v.clamp with (calling Vector2.splat with -1.0), (calling Vector2.splat with 1.0)
    ## ```

    @mutates
    weave clamp with lo as raylib.Vector2, hi as raylib.Vector2 into raylib.Vector2:
        return calling raymath.Vector2Clamp with (essence of self), lo, hi

    ## Clamps the length of the receiver between `lo` and `hi`.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `lo`: minimum length.
    ## - `hi`: maximum length.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The receiver, with its magnitude inside `[lo, hi]` and its direction unchanged.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation; the receiver is rewritten in place.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 10.0, 0.0
    ## calling v.clamp_magnitude with 0.0, 2.0   # v is (2, 0)
    ## ```

    @mutates
    weave clamp_magnitude with lo as f32, hi as f32 into raylib.Vector2:
        return calling raymath.Vector2ClampValue with (essence of self), lo, hi

    ## Moves the receiver linearly towards `other`.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `other`: the target vector.
    ## - `t`: blend factor; `0` keeps `self`, `1` yields `other`.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The receiver, interpolated towards `other`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation; the receiver is rewritten in place.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 0.0, 0.0
    ## calling v.lerp_to with (calling Vector2.splat with 10.0), 0.5   # v is (5, 5)
    ## ```

    @mutates
    weave lerp_to with other as raylib.Vector2, t as f32 into raylib.Vector2:
        return calling raymath.Vector2Lerp with (essence of self), other, t

    ## Moves the receiver towards `target` by at most `max_distance`.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `target`: the destination vector.
    ## - `max_distance`: the step length.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The receiver, moved towards `target` without overshooting it.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation; the receiver is rewritten in place.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 0.0, 0.0
    ## calling v.move_towards with (calling Vector2.new with 10.0, 0.0), 1.0
    ## ```

    @mutates
    weave move_towards with target as raylib.Vector2, max_distance as f32 into raylib.Vector2:
        return calling raymath.Vector2MoveTowards with (essence of self), target, max_distance

    ## Rotates the receiver around the origin.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `angle`: rotation in radians.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The receiver, rotated by `angle`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation; the receiver is rewritten in place.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 1.0, 0.0
    ## calling v.rotate_by with 1.5707963   # v is approximately (0, 1)
    ## ```

    @mutates
    weave rotate_by with angle as f32 into raylib.Vector2:
        return calling raymath.Vector2Rotate with (essence of self), angle

    ## Returns the length (magnitude) of the vector.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The Euclidean length as `f32`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 3.0, 4.0
    ## var n as f32 is calling v.length   # 5.0
    ## ```

    weave length into f32:
        return calling raymath.Vector2Length with (essence of self)

    ## Returns the squared length of the vector.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - Cheaper than `length` when only a comparison is needed: it avoids the square root.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The squared magnitude as `f32`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 3.0, 4.0
    ## var n as f32 is calling v.length_sq   # 25.0
    ## ```

    weave length_sq into f32:
        return calling raymath.Vector2LengthSqr with (essence of self)

    ## Returns the dot product of the vector and `other`.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `other`: the second vector.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The scalar dot product.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var a as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 1.0, 0.0
    ## var b as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 0.0, 1.0
    ## var d as f32 is calling a.dot with b   # 0.0
    ## ```

    weave dot with other as raylib.Vector2 into f32:
        return calling raymath.Vector2DotProduct with (essence of self), other

    ## Returns the 2D cross product (a scalar) of the vector and `other`.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `other`: the second vector.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The signed area of the parallelogram, as `f32`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var a as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 1.0, 0.0
    ## var b as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 0.0, 1.0
    ## var c as f32 is calling a.cross with b   # 1.0
    ## ```

    weave cross with other as raylib.Vector2 into f32:
        return calling raymath.Vector2CrossProduct with (essence of self), other

    ## Returns the distance between the vector and `other`.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `other`: the second point.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The Euclidean distance as `f32`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var a as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 0.0, 0.0
    ## var b as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 3.0, 4.0
    ## var d as f32 is calling a.distance_to with b   # 5.0
    ## ```

    weave distance_to with other as raylib.Vector2 into f32:
        return calling raymath.Vector2Distance with (essence of self), other

    ## Returns the squared distance between the vector and `other`.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - Avoids the square root when only a comparison is needed.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The squared distance as `f32`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var a as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 0.0, 0.0
    ## var b as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 3.0, 4.0
    ## var d as f32 is calling a.distance_sq_to with b   # 25.0
    ## ```

    weave distance_sq_to with other as raylib.Vector2 into f32:
        return calling raymath.Vector2DistanceSqr with (essence of self), other

    ## Returns the signed angle from the vector to `other`.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - The coordinate system is Y-down: positive angles appear clockwise.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The signed angle in radians.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var a as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 1.0, 0.0
    ## var b as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 0.0, 1.0
    ## var ang as f32 is calling a.angle_to with b
    ## ```

    weave angle_to with other as raylib.Vector2 into f32:
        return calling raymath.Vector2Angle with (essence of self), other

    ## Returns the angle of the line defined by the vector and `other`.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - Both vectors are expected to be normalised, as in the C function.
    ## - The C routine computes `-atan2(dy, dx)`, so the result follows the Y-down convention and is the negation of the usual angle.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The line angle in radians.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## No allocation.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var a as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 1.0, 0.0
    ## var b as raylib.Vector2 is calling Vector2.new with 0.0, 1.0
    ## var ang as f32 is calling a.angle_line_to with b
    ## ```

    weave angle_line_to with other as raylib.Vector2 into f32:
        return calling raymath.Vector2LineAngle with (essence of self), other

    ## Returns the component-wise minimum of the vector and `other`.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `other`: the vector to compare against.
    ##
